# Javadoc: type vs method/constructor (Checkstyle)

The build runs Checkstyle `JavadocType` on types (classes, interfaces, enums, `record` declarations) and `JavadocMethod` on methods and constructors.

## Classes, interfaces, enums — do not use `@param` on type-level Javadoc

**Invalid for this codebase (fails with `Unused @param tag for '…' [JavadocType]`):**

```java
/**
 * Something.
 *
 * @param foo description   // WRONG here — not a method/constructor
 */
public class MyDto {
```

**Correct patterns:**

1. **Put `@param` on the constructor** that takes those values (preferred when you have an all-args or primary constructor).
2. **Use field Javadoc** (`/** ... */` above each field) when fields are not constructor parameters.
3. **Use a short type summary only** (`/** ... */` on the class) with no `@param` lines.

## Records — document components on the type (Checkstyle 13.9+)

From **Checkstyle 13.9**, `JavadocType` expects `@param` tags on the **record type** Javadoc for each
record component (missing tags fail the build). That is the opposite of the class/enum rule above.

```java
/**
 * Claim snapshot.
 *
 * @param enrollmentId enrollment that claimed the attempt
 * @param authAttemptId claimed auth attempt id
 * @since 2026
 */
public record ClaimedPendingAuth(Integer enrollmentId, Integer authAttemptId) {}
```

Factory methods and explicit constructors still use `@param` under `JavadocMethod` as usual.

## Why this repeats

Authors often mirror “parameter list” style on ordinary classes. For **classes / interfaces /
enums**, Checkstyle still treats type-level `@param` as unused. For **records**, Checkstyle 13.9+
treats component `@param` tags on the type as required — follow that exception.
